{"batchId":"R01_B02","researchUnitId":"CLT_DINE_NAVAJO","personalityId":"EXILE_FORCED_MIGRATION_WOUND","personalityBridge":"Eidolon-authored inference from a specific 1864 state action: the U.S. government forced Diné people to march from their homeland to Bosque Redondo. The mapping preserves perpetrator, victim, date, route, and forced character; it does not essentialize Diné psychology.","inferenceClassification":"EIDOLON_AUTHORED_INFERENCE","terrainBroad":["DESERT","MOUNTAIN"],"terrainSpecific":["PLATEAU","WOODLAND"],"journalEntryIds":{"personalityId":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_DINE_NAVAJO_PERSONALITY_ID_001","terrainBroad":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_DINE_NAVAJO_TERRAIN_BROAD_001","terrainSpecific":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_DINE_NAVAJO_TERRAIN_SPECIFIC_001"},"status":"SIMULATION_READY"}
{"batchId":"R01_B02","researchUnitId":"CLT_HAUDENOSAUNEE","personalityId":"REPAIR_DESTROYED_INFRASTRUCTURE_WOUND","personalityBridge":"Eidolon-authored inference from the Continental Army's 1779 destruction of Haudenosaunee villages, houses, fields, orchards, and food stores. The mapping represents documented damage requiring material recovery, not a timeless group character.","inferenceClassification":"EIDOLON_AUTHORED_INFERENCE","terrainBroad":["FRESHWATER"],"terrainSpecific":["RIVER","LAKE"],"journalEntryIds":{"personalityId":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_HAUDENOSAUNEE_PERSONALITY_ID_001","terrainBroad":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_HAUDENOSAUNEE_TERRAIN_BROAD_001","terrainSpecific":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_HAUDENOSAUNEE_TERRAIN_SPECIFIC_001"},"status":"SIMULATION_READY"}
{"batchId":"R01_B02","researchUnitId":"CLT_HOPI","personalityId":"AUTHENTICITY_ASSIMILATION_WOUND","personalityBridge":"Eidolon-authored inference from a named late-19th-century U.S. policy to Americanize Hopi people, coerce children into schools, and extinguish Hopi culture. The mapping preserves the external actor and imposed assimilation; it is not a claim about inherent Hopi personality.","inferenceClassification":"EIDOLON_AUTHORED_INFERENCE","terrainBroad":["DESERT","GRASSLAND","FOREST"],"terrainSpecific":["PLATEAU","WOODLAND"],"journalEntryIds":{"personalityId":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_HOPI_PERSONALITY_ID_001","terrainBroad":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_HOPI_TERRAIN_BROAD_001","terrainSpecific":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_HOPI_TERRAIN_SPECIFIC_001"},"status":"SIMULATION_READY"}
{"batchId":"R01_B02","researchUnitId":"CLT_MISSISSIPPIAN_EASTERN_WOODLANDS","personalityId":"COLLECTIVE_MEMORY_TECHNIQUE_TRADITION","personalityBridge":"Eidolon-authored inference from documented technique continuity: Mississippian communities repeatedly built earthen ceremonial mounds, and Natchez and other communities continued a traditional Mississippian way of life into the colonial period. This maps an evidenced cultural technique and continuity, not an ethnic psychological trait.","inferenceClassification":"EIDOLON_AUTHORED_INFERENCE","terrainBroad":["FRESHWATER"],"terrainSpecific":["RIVER","FLOODPLAIN"],"journalEntryIds":{"personalityId":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_MISSISSIPPIAN_EASTERN_WOODLANDS_PERSONALITY_ID_001","terrainBroad":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_MISSISSIPPIAN_EASTERN_WOODLANDS_TERRAIN_BROAD_001","terrainSpecific":"JRN_R01_B02_CLT_MISSISSIPPIAN_EASTERN_WOODLANDS_TERRAIN_SPECIFIC_001"},"status":"SIMULATION_READY"}
